00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:21.150
let's start with a
quick look at Cloud SQL.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.550
Cloud SQL is a managed
database service

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:28.350
that offers MySQL, SQL
Server, and PostgreSQL.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.420
This means that if you
have an application that

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.520
uses MySQL, SQL
Server, or PostgreSQL,

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.250
it will work with Cloud SQL.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:40.170
Cloud SQL focuses on managing
mundane administration aspects

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:41.180
of these databases.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:43.170
And I'll talk about
that a little bit more.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.720
Beyond just application
compatibility,

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:49.590
it means that Cloud SQL is
compatible with your favorite

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:51.150
database administration tools.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:53.280
So if you're a SQL
Server user, for example,

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.520
I encourage you to take a look
at the links on this slide.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.370
Here, we're talking about
how SQL Server users can

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:02.430
use their favorite
administration tool, Cloud SQL,

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:04.860
incorporating the
Cloud SQL proxy, which

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.910
makes Cloud SQL instances in the
cloud look local to any machine

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:10.313
that you're working on.

00:02:10.313 --> 00:02:11.730
Follow up on those
links if you're

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.270
interested in learning more.

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:16.220
When I think about
running a database,

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:19.550
this tends to be the technology
stack or the diagram of a stack

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:20.840
that I think of.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.570
At the leftmost
column, we see things

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:26.120
related to running a data
center or a server environment

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:26.960
in general.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.770
This means server maintenance,
racking and stacking, power,

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:32.510
HVAC, and networking--
things that

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:34.880
go into a data center or
any server environment.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:38.030
Of course, this is taken
care of for you in the Cloud

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:40.855
where with Cloud SQL,
when we offer an instance

00:02:40.855 --> 00:02:44.450
and you configure the number
of vCPU cores for RAM,

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:46.610
we make sure that you get
a consistent experience,

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:49.610
that number of cores in RAM,
even as our underlying hardware

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:50.780
changes.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:52.550
Moving one column
to the right, we

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:53.930
look at the operating system--

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:56.240
both installation and
patching or keeping

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.370
the operating system up to date.

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:00.620
One more column to the right
is finally the database.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:02.690
Here we're installing
the database.

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:05.660
We're keeping it up to date
with its own set of patches.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:09.500
And we're starting to protect
data with scheduled backups.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:11.730
And then rightmost
in the diagram

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:14.450
are things like high
availability, which usually

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:18.080
includes some health checking
and maybe automatic failover,

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.110
as well as scalability where
we're using often replication

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:23.560
to scale out reads to replicas.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:25.580
We want to wrap this
whole technology stack

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:27.950
in monitoring so that
if there is a problem,

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:31.010
we're able to quickly
find the root cause.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:34.260
Cloud SQL's job is take
this stack or this diagram

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.770
that you see, what it really
takes to run a database,

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:40.870
and wrap all of that
in an API, CLI, and UI

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:43.880
so that for your application
teams in your environment

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:47.630
or your developers, they can
easily start up and create

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:50.480
a dev or test database,
which might be very simple,

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:53.730
but just as easily create a
production-grade database,

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:57.530
which means it includes high
availability, which relies

00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:00.500
on synchronous replication
and automatic failover,

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:03.650
includes a backup plan
for data protection,

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:06.560
and even cross-region
replication for disaster

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:07.280
recovery.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:10.280
Again, all of these
wrapped in an API, CLI,

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:12.470
or UI that means in
just a few minutes,

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:15.470
you can get this set up.

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:16.760
Tim sums this up well.

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:19.130
And Tim Kelton is a
customer at Descartes Labs.

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:22.430
He says Cloud SQL gives
his team a bit more time

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:26.060
to work on the products that
provide value to his customers.

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:27.650
His team has decided
they're going

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:30.530
to spend less time on
certain aspects of database

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:32.900
administration and turn
those over to Cloud SQL

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:34.910
so they can focus
their time elsewhere

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:37.760
on products that are more
valuable to their customers.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:39.690
I think Tim sums that up well.

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:42.830
And one thing to note here
is that the diagram I showed

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:46.145
and what's also backing Tim's
quote is a dedicated SRE

00:04:46.145 --> 00:04:48.590
team, Sight Reliability
Engineering Team for Cloud

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:51.980
SQL that is on call 24 by 7.

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:54.380
These are the folks who
write the automation to make

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:57.050
sure our service is
healthy and runs well.

00:04:57.050 --> 00:04:58.550
And when automation
fails, they're

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:00.530
also the folks who put
hands on a keyboard

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:03.760
to get a database
back up and running.

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:05.510
Now, that we've taken
a look at Cloud SQL,

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:07.820
we'll start to introduce
what's new, what have we been

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:09.300
up to in the past few months.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:12.020
And I think what you'll find is
we have great momentum in terms

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:13.305
of enterprise features.

00:05:13.305 --> 00:05:14.930
We're building on an
enterprise feature

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:16.790
set with a number of
these new features

00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:18.330
that we'll cover today.

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:20.300
First, let's find
some flexibility

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:22.160
in your database environment.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:25.700
We'll start with BigQuery
to Cloud SQL federation.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:29.960
This enhancement gives analysts
a way to quickly reduce time

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:32.510
to value for business insight.

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:34.940
Perhaps, they want to explore
some data without setting up

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:36.770
a pipeline first,
or perhaps, they

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:38.660
have a quick question
they need to answer.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:41.270
And that data might be spread
across multiple Cloud SQL

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:43.670
instances and even
other data sources.

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:46.070
With BigQuery-to-Cloud
SQL federation,

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:48.830
the analyst can query one
or more Cloud SQL instances

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:51.720
and join that data with
data that already resides

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:54.530
in BigQuery or
perhaps resides in one

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:57.813
of the other external data
sources supported by BigQuery.

00:05:57.813 --> 00:06:00.230
What you see in this slide is
a depiction of a very simple

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:02.390
query an analyst is
running in BigQuery

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:04.520
where they're using
the external query

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:07.280
function, which is what
we use the ask Cloud

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:09.160
SQL to go run a query.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:11.340
Here the queries
that are in Cloud SQL

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:12.980
has been input by
the analyst, which

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:14.540
means we're pushing
down the query

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:17.370
or asking Cloud SQL to run the
aggregates, for example, so

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:20.480
that we're not sending back
blindly a large table of data.

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:23.810
The data sent back from
Cloud SQL is in table format

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:26.060
and can then be joined
with other Cloud SQL data,

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:29.070
whether it be Q data or data
from another external source

00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:30.950
supported by BigQuery.

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:33.710
All of this can be done
without setting up a pipeline.

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:36.950
And once an analyst is sure
that the insights being provided

00:06:36.950 --> 00:06:38.990
are useful, from
there, the team can

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:43.900
decide to build pipelines for
a regular reporting schedule.

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:46.090
Next, we'll look at
committed use discounts.

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:49.420
And we don't often think
of pricing and flexibility

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:50.753
in the same sentence.

00:06:50.753 --> 00:06:52.420
Hopefully, what you'll
find is that what

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:54.503
we've done with Cloud with
committed use discounts

00:06:54.503 --> 00:06:55.870
is indeed flexible.

00:06:55.870 --> 00:07:00.040
A committed use discount
is ideal for a workload

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:02.150
with predictable resource needs.

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:04.900
This is a way of saying that
they're great for environments

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:08.575
where you're running a number
of Cloud SQL instances 24 by 7.

00:07:08.575 --> 00:07:11.200
We know that each month, you're
going to spend a certain amount

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:12.460
on Cloud SQL.

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:15.670
When you commit to a
one-year or three-year term

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:18.910
with a committed use discount,
or a CUD as I'll call them,

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:23.830
you receive either a 25%
or a 52% discount, one year

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:25.420
and three year.

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:28.780
The flexibility comes in
with committed use discounts

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:30.820
in that these are
easily and fully

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:33.100
transferable between
the database engines

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:37.810
supported by Cloud SQL, such
as MySQL, PostgreSQL, as well

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:39.100
as SQL Server.

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:41.770
The committed use
discounts or CUDs discount

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:45.310
against the vCPUs
or CPU cores and RAM

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:48.010
associated with each of
your Cloud SQL instances.

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:50.350
And here, if your
database's state changes,

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:52.900
let's say from SQL Server
to Postgres over time,

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:53.920
it's no problem.

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:57.100
Your CUDs will continue to
apply and you don't even

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:58.540
need to reach out to us.

00:07:58.540 --> 00:08:00.700
Not only are these
fully transferable,

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:03.100
but you don't need to
take any specific action.

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:04.990
You purchase a
committed use discount

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:08.950
in a currency denomination,
for example, dollars.

00:08:08.950 --> 00:08:11.470
And we charge your
instance rates

00:08:11.470 --> 00:08:13.840
against that
commitment each month.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:16.810
These are also fully
transferable between machine

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:17.590
sizes.

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:20.410
So you don't need to
forecast, which machine size

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:22.210
or how many CPUs
per machine you'll

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:23.950
be needing in the future.

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:26.210
You simply use CPUs
as you see fit.

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:28.390
They'll count against your
committed use discount.

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:29.830
In this way, your
database's state

00:08:29.830 --> 00:08:32.860
can change quite dramatically
over, say, a three year period

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:35.320
of time without any
problems applying

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:37.059
to committed use discounts.

00:08:37.059 --> 00:08:40.900
We hope this will make your
planning for committed use

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:43.620
discounts much easier with
a lot less forecasting

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:48.870
and that you find the whole
process to be indeed flexible.

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:52.560
Looking now at how to protect
against the unexpected,

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:55.500
we'll start with
disaster recovery.

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:59.460
In Google Cloud terms, we
often categorize disasters

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:01.230
as a region outage.

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:03.450
A region in Google
Cloud is comprised

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:06.860
of three or more zones, where
a zone is a separate failure

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:07.740
domain.

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:10.560
This means that if we
have a problem in Zone A,

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:15.270
it usually doesn't affect Zone B
or Zone C, so a guard in itself

00:09:15.270 --> 00:09:16.620
against a regional outage.

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:19.940
But if a regional outage were
to occur where two or more

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:22.150
of the zones in the
region are down,

00:09:22.150 --> 00:09:25.170
we have introduced a
cross-region replica

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:27.960
enhancement, or cross-region
replication enhancement that

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:30.270
allows you to guard against
this sort of outage,

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:32.430
a full-region outage.

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:34.200
With a cross-region
replica, you're

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:36.690
able to ensure that you
have a near-real-time copy

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:39.840
of your data in any
region around the world

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:42.000
other than in your
primary region.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.760
We're using Google's
global network

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:47.400
as well as Google
Cloud's VPCs, which

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:50.550
are global in nature spanning
all regions in the world,

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:53.310
to ensure that this
experience is easy to setup.

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:57.820
And it can be made very secure
and private via our VPC option.

00:09:57.820 --> 00:10:00.570
So what you see in this
slide, as in the screenshot,

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:02.940
that I'm selecting
a region, something

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:04.890
other than where I have
my primary instance.

00:10:04.890 --> 00:10:08.520
So if my primary instance is
in, for example, US Central 1,

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:12.500
here I'm selecting US West 1
as my region for the replica.

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:14.760
And that's really
about all I have to do.

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:17.460
This will now take advantage
of my private VPC network.

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:20.220
So I know I'm securely
transferring data or copying

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:22.980
data, rather, in near-real
time to my replica.

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:25.920
And the replica is now
available in another region

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:27.490
than my primary instance.

00:10:27.490 --> 00:10:28.950
This means in the
event that I have

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:32.070
a outage in my primary
region, US Central 1,

00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:34.160
I know I have a
near-real-time dataset that

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:36.810
can be made available very
quickly in another region

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:39.030
in case of that disaster.

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:42.630
Of note here is that
this replica is also

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:43.530
a standard replica.

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:46.170
Meaning if you have users
in another region who

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:48.630
need low-latency
reads to a replica,

00:10:48.630 --> 00:10:51.420
or you want to run reporting,
for example, in the replica,

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:54.510
you can certainly do so, knowing
that the replica is still

00:10:54.510 --> 00:10:57.790
available for disaster
recovery purposes.

00:10:57.790 --> 00:11:00.430
We talk a lot about disasters
and widespread outages.

00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:02.860
You all have done a lot
of disaster planning

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:06.130
as part of your careers in IT.

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:09.310
What we tend to realize,
though, is often,

00:11:09.310 --> 00:11:12.280
the more common event
is a user error.

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:14.920
We are sort of the
disaster, if you will.

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:18.040
I might drop a table or change
a row of data by mistake

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:20.440
and I want an Undo
button to get back

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:22.660
in time before I
changed that data,

00:11:22.660 --> 00:11:24.490
so I get back to where I was.

00:11:24.490 --> 00:11:28.680
We've long had point-in-time
recovery for our MySQL product

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:29.650
in Cloud SQL.

00:11:29.650 --> 00:11:33.280
We're now proud to introduce
it for PostgreSQL as well.

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:37.390
With PostgreSQL, we store
transaction logs or WALs

00:11:37.390 --> 00:11:39.670
for the past seven
days, which allow you

00:11:39.670 --> 00:11:42.520
as a user to select a
millisecond at any point

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:43.860
in the last seven days.

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:46.510
So if I made a mistake on a
Friday afternoon, for example,

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:48.588
and I don't realize it
until Monday morning,

00:11:48.588 --> 00:11:51.130
it's easy for me to get back to
the millisecond or the second

00:11:51.130 --> 00:11:54.400
before I made that mistake on
Friday and recover that data.

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:57.220
It gives me an Undo button,
as I mentioned, and helps

00:11:57.220 --> 00:12:00.100
to protect against the
more common issue, which

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:01.300
is user error.

00:12:04.550 --> 00:12:09.110
These features add to Cloud
SQL's enterprise-ready list

00:12:09.110 --> 00:12:11.410
for data protection
and disaster recovery.

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:13.670
And I think it's
worth summarizing

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:17.490
how to build a production
instance with Cloud SQL.

00:12:17.490 --> 00:12:20.280
We start by enabling
automatic daily backups.

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:21.680
And I'd encourage
you to consider

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:25.550
taking more frequent on-demand
backups, especially when you're

00:12:25.550 --> 00:12:27.780
making a major change
to your database.

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:29.975
For example, you might be
making a schema change.

00:12:29.975 --> 00:12:32.077
It's a great time to
take an on-demand backup

00:12:32.077 --> 00:12:34.160
just before you make that
change, in case anything

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:35.317
goes wrong.

00:12:35.317 --> 00:12:37.650
I'd encourage you also to
enable point-in-time recovery,

00:12:37.650 --> 00:12:39.650
so that you have
very granular restore

00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:42.140
options for the last seven
days as the example was

00:12:42.140 --> 00:12:43.340
with PostgreSQL.

00:12:43.340 --> 00:12:46.250
You can get back now to
any millisecond in time

00:12:46.250 --> 00:12:48.680
in case of a user error.

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:51.130
We also have an export
mechanism in Cloud SQL that

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:54.090
allows you to export your
data in a portable format

00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:56.573
to Google Cloud Storage, or GCS.

00:12:56.573 --> 00:12:58.990
This can be a multi-region
bucket, a single-region bucket,

00:12:58.990 --> 00:12:59.920
or otherwise.

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:01.510
When you use
multi-region buckets,

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:03.700
it ensures that in
a regional outage,

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:06.220
your data is automatically
replicated and stored

00:13:06.220 --> 00:13:08.540
in another region so you
can gain access to it.

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:12.080
You can also store these exports
in any other object storage,

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:14.550
meaning on premises or in
another cloud provider.

00:13:14.550 --> 00:13:17.260
It allows you a very
portable format of your data,

00:13:17.260 --> 00:13:20.770
so that you can take
it where you want.

00:13:20.770 --> 00:13:23.773
In region, ensure that you
enable high availability.

00:13:23.773 --> 00:13:25.190
High availability
tells Cloud SQL,

00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:27.020
the high-availability
configuration,

00:13:27.020 --> 00:13:29.050
tells Cloud SQL to
do automatic health

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:31.360
checking of your
instance and to set up

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:33.270
a synchronous
replication between zones

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:35.650
so that in the
event of a problem,

00:13:35.650 --> 00:13:37.600
we automatically fail
over on your behalf,

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:41.350
bringing your database back
up even after a major fault.

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:44.710
This is necessary for the SLA
and generally good availability

00:13:44.710 --> 00:13:46.870
guidance for any
production system.

00:13:46.870 --> 00:13:50.650
Lastly, as I mentioned, to
protect against a region

00:13:50.650 --> 00:13:54.700
outage, consider using our
cross-region replication

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:56.500
or cross-region replicas.

00:13:56.500 --> 00:13:58.720
Again, you'll have a
near-real-time copy,

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:01.150
asynchronous
replication, which means

00:14:01.150 --> 00:14:03.700
you'll have data available
to you in another region

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:05.500
should your primary
region have an outage.

00:14:05.500 --> 00:14:07.490
And you can make
that data accessible

00:14:07.490 --> 00:14:11.650
very quickly to applications
to get back online.

00:14:11.650 --> 00:14:14.650
Looking now at how to get
automatic transparency

00:14:14.650 --> 00:14:16.660
and trusted security
controls, we'll

00:14:16.660 --> 00:14:19.120
start with Access Transparency.

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:22.360
Access Transparency gives
you near-real-time logs

00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:24.250
when a Google
Cloud administrator

00:14:24.250 --> 00:14:26.900
accesses your content.

00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:28.910
Cloud audit logs already
provide visibility

00:14:28.910 --> 00:14:31.400
into the actions of
your own administrators.

00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:32.960
So for example,
your administrators

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:34.950
might be creating a
Cloud SQL instance,

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:36.890
taking a few
backups via our API.

00:14:36.890 --> 00:14:38.450
All this is logged today.

00:14:38.450 --> 00:14:42.110
However, this audit trail
typically or previously stopped

00:14:42.110 --> 00:14:46.280
once your cloud provider support
or engineering team is engaged.

00:14:46.280 --> 00:14:48.840
We previously didn't
log those transactions.

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:52.940
Now when you open a support
ticket with our support team,

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:55.490
with via Access
Transparency, you'll

00:14:55.490 --> 00:14:57.830
see their actions
in your audit log

00:14:57.830 --> 00:15:01.040
as well for our manual and
targeted accesses-- again,

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:03.470
either by support or engineering
to get your database back

00:15:03.470 --> 00:15:04.710
online.

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:07.280
You can take this further
with Access Approval.

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:10.310
So beyond just logging
and viewing the access,

00:15:10.310 --> 00:15:12.740
you can use the Access
Approval feature

00:15:12.740 --> 00:15:15.290
to increase your
level of control

00:15:15.290 --> 00:15:19.650
by requiring explicit
approvals before Google support

00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:22.610
or engineering can access
your data or configurations

00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:25.650
on Google Cloud.

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:28.530
I recommend taking a look
at both Access Transparency

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:32.580
and Access Approval to see how
they fit with your environment.

00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:34.620
Next up, let's
look at encryption,

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:36.300
and specifically
customer-managed

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:37.680
encryption keys.

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:40.050
These keys are intended
for organizations

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:42.720
that have sensitive
or regulated data that

00:15:42.720 --> 00:15:45.870
requires them to manage
their own encryption keys.

00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:49.380
These keys are stored in
a key management system

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:50.430
that you manage.

00:15:50.430 --> 00:15:52.980
We're using keys in
Cloud SQL that you manage

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:56.070
and we access them as
part of our operations.

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:58.290
Should you remove
access from that key,

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:00.840
then when Cloud SQL performs
a major operation next,

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:03.700
it won't be able to get the
key and access to that system

00:16:03.700 --> 00:16:04.770
is shut down.

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:08.400
Again, this feature allows
you to use cryptographic keys

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:10.950
that you manage to control
your data at rest--

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:15.350
the data we store in Cloud SQL.

00:16:15.350 --> 00:16:19.300
I mentioned earlier Google
Cloud's global VPCs.

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:21.040
We've introduced in
Cloud SQL support

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:23.920
for VPC Service Controls.

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:28.750
These generally in GCP allow
you to define very fine-grained

00:16:28.750 --> 00:16:32.200
perimeter controls across
numerous Google Cloud Services,

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:34.620
of course, including Cloud SQL.

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:36.910
For Cloud SQL, this
pairs very well

00:16:36.910 --> 00:16:40.840
with VPCs or private
IP addresses.

00:16:40.840 --> 00:16:44.680
With the combination of private
IP addresses and VPC Service

00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:47.830
Controls, you're now able to
make private your Cloud SQL

00:16:47.830 --> 00:16:50.890
instances both from a data
path perspective as well

00:16:50.890 --> 00:16:52.810
as a control path perspective.

00:16:52.810 --> 00:16:56.620
The private IP or VPC allows
you to protect the data path--

00:16:56.620 --> 00:16:59.680
the thing that your clients and
applications are connected to--

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:04.079
by using a private IP address
on a VPC that you control.

00:17:04.079 --> 00:17:06.640
VPC service controls
for Cloud SQL

00:17:06.640 --> 00:17:09.130
protect the
administrative API, who

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:11.230
can create Cloud
SQL instances, who

00:17:11.230 --> 00:17:14.140
can take backups via the
API or the UI, for example.

00:17:14.140 --> 00:17:16.119
You're now able to
make our administration

00:17:16.119 --> 00:17:20.470
API totally private as well,
ensuring that you can control

00:17:20.470 --> 00:17:23.829
where your teams can access
Cloud SQL instances, not

00:17:23.829 --> 00:17:26.260
just on the data path,
but also on the control

00:17:26.260 --> 00:17:29.160
path of the admin API.

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:33.180
Let's round out with how to stay
up to date without the risks.

00:17:33.180 --> 00:17:36.990
As a reminder, Cloud SQL
has long offered maintenance

00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:41.250
windows, which are
one-hour-per-week times when

00:17:41.250 --> 00:17:43.650
you define that Google
can perform maintenance

00:17:43.650 --> 00:17:44.790
on your instance.

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:47.340
In general, Cloud SQL
typically performs

00:17:47.340 --> 00:17:49.350
maintenance once a
quarter on your instances

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:51.660
to apply patches and
keep things up to date.

00:17:51.660 --> 00:17:54.360
We would adhere to
your maintenance window

00:17:54.360 --> 00:17:55.808
as you see in this diagram.

00:17:55.808 --> 00:17:57.600
One thing to note about
maintenance windows

00:17:57.600 --> 00:17:59.670
is that we offer
certain timings.

00:17:59.670 --> 00:18:03.180
If in a given project you
have dev and test database

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:06.360
instances, as well as your
production database instances,

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:08.820
you might put the dev
and test databases

00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:10.740
on the earlier timing.

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:14.250
And you might put the production
databases on the later timing.

00:18:14.250 --> 00:18:18.450
This ensures then that the dev
test instances on the earlier

00:18:18.450 --> 00:18:20.940
timing get maintenance
or receive maintenance

00:18:20.940 --> 00:18:23.315
about a week in advance of
your production instances.

00:18:23.315 --> 00:18:25.440
That way, it gives you a
chance to test the updates

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:27.773
with the application before
they go into your production

00:18:27.773 --> 00:18:28.890
environment.

00:18:28.890 --> 00:18:33.210
We've recently added maintenance
notification and rescheduling.

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:35.903
When you opt in,
we'll email you a week

00:18:35.903 --> 00:18:37.320
in advance of
maintenance, letting

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:39.445
you know that we're planning
to perform maintenance

00:18:39.445 --> 00:18:40.870
in your next maintenance window.

00:18:40.870 --> 00:18:43.230
You get a few options as
part of this notification,

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:44.940
including the
ability to reschedule

00:18:44.940 --> 00:18:47.430
to a following window
one week later,

00:18:47.430 --> 00:18:49.890
or the ability to apply
maintenance anytime

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:51.660
you click the Apply Now button.

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:54.360
You can also set a specific
time, any time in the next two

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:56.130
weeks, to apply
maintenance if you're not

00:18:56.130 --> 00:18:58.560
happy with your current
maintenance window schedule.

00:18:58.560 --> 00:19:02.040
This gives you much more
control as to when maintenance

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:04.440
is applied, especially on
your production instances,

00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:07.380
and lets your teams know well
in advance that maintenance

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:09.590
will be performed.

00:19:09.590 --> 00:19:12.740
To wrap up, hopefully
these features

00:19:12.740 --> 00:19:15.300
help you find a bit more
flexibility in your environment

00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:18.230
with things like BigQuery
federation and committed use

00:19:18.230 --> 00:19:20.960
discounts; help you protect
against the unexpected,

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:24.020
be that user error with
point-in-time recovery,

00:19:24.020 --> 00:19:27.110
or a regional outage with
cross-region replication

00:19:27.110 --> 00:19:29.040
and disaster recovery.

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:31.260
They'll allow you to get
automatic transparency

00:19:31.260 --> 00:19:34.230
and trusted security controls,
things like private IP

00:19:34.230 --> 00:19:37.800
and now the addition of VPC
security controls, for example,

00:19:37.800 --> 00:19:40.350
as well as customer-managed
encryption keys.

00:19:40.350 --> 00:19:43.140
And we'll round it out with our
new maintenance notifications

00:19:43.140 --> 00:19:45.210
and rescheduling functionality.

00:19:45.210 --> 00:19:47.700
As you can see, Cloud
SQL is continuing

00:19:47.700 --> 00:19:50.610
to build its momentum with
respect to enterprise features.

00:19:50.610 --> 00:19:54.380
And you can expect to see more
of this in future offerings.

00:19:54.380 --> 00:19:56.520
For next steps, I encourage
you to take a look

00:19:56.520 --> 00:19:57.750
